data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Phase two processing verifies intact records identified in phase one, iterating through each to validate payload layouts and data types according to predefined rules for each record type. Each byte is checked to ensure it falls within defined limits, with specific logic applied to each record type. Invalid records trigger error conditions based on defined constraints.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R14 focuses on transaction ID validation. For types other than 8, a 32-bit transaction ID must be non-zero. For type 8, a count is read, and subsequent IDs must also be non-zero. Violations trigger an error.\n\nR15 validates keys for PUT, DELETE, and READ types. Key lengths must be within a defined range, and all key bytes must fall within a specified ASCII range. Invalid key bytes result in an error.\n\nR16 handles value validation for PUT and READ types based on the key length. For PUT, the value is extracted from the payload; for READ when a flag is set, it extracts from the payload. A check ensures value bytes are within specified ASCII limits; invalid bytes result in error.\n\nR16a validates names for SAVEPOINT and ROLLBACK records. Name length must be within defined limits.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R17 addresses transaction ID reuse, specifically for BEGIN records. A check verifies if the transaction ID is already present in a set of seen transactions; if so, a duplicate transaction error is triggered.\n\nR18 enforces an open transaction limit. If the number of open transactions reaches the maximum allowed, a \"too many open\" error is signaled.\n\nR19 checks for open transactions for certain record types like PUT, DELETE, COMMIT, and READ. If the transaction ID isn't found in a map of open transactions, a \"transaction not open\" error is thrown.\n\nR19a imposes a write limit within transactions for PUT and DELETE record types, raising a transaction too large error if the write count exceeds the maximum.\n\nR19b checks for the existence of savepoints during rollback operations, signaling a \"no savepoint\" error if the named savepoint is missing.\n\nR19c involves sorting the keys of `openTxns` for CHECKPOINT records.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The checkpoint verification focuses on the correct ordering and presence of transaction IDs. The `openTxns` map's keys must be sorted and checked for equality against a checkpoint record's listing. The read check considers a key and its value based on a transactional view, examining writes (PUT, DELETE) and a snapshot. It verifies the presence or absence of the value, throwing a mismatch if expectations are not met based on a flag.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The read check validates a key and value against a transactional snapshot, triggering a mismatch error if the value does not match the expected state. Moving on to the *Actions* section, BEGIN records will add transactions to `seenTxns` and `openTxns`, storing transaction details including a snapshot of the current state. PUT and DELETE records append write operations to the current transaction's write list.\n\nCOMMIT records will be carefully reviewed for conflicts with other committed transactions based on overlap in written keys and read sets. It seems like the agent needs to maintain a list of committed transactions, to track conflicts at a larger offset than the begin record. A detailed verification of this conflict detection logic is needed.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

