data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Considering transaction conflict detection for COMMIT records, the focus is on overlap between a committing transaction's read set and previously committed transactions' written keys.  The logic clarifies written keys include PUT and DELETE operations, even those subsequently rolled back.  Confirming U != T because of log ordering and unique transaction IDs, the check proceeds if the current transaction has a non-empty read set.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The commit logic examines each committed transaction (`u`) against the current transaction (`t`) to detect read-write conflicts, setting a flag if any overlap is found between `u`'s written keys and `t`'s read set. If no conflicts are found, the transaction's writes (PUT, DELETE) are applied to the state, and a `CommittedTxn` object is added to the history; the transaction ID is added to `committed`. If conflicts exist, the writes are discarded, and the transaction is added to `aborted`.\n\nAbort operations remove the transaction from open transactions and add the transaction ID to the `aborted` list. Savepoints are created by storing the current write list size and record offset. Rollback truncates the transaction's write list to a stored savepoint position.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-15811bb4-c3a0-4dac-858f-a130101ddc55","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795545140,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The analysis focuses on the final result construction. The `state` is built from a deterministic `LinkedHashMap` for predictable key order. `committed` and `aborted` lists are copied for immutability. The `incomplete` list extracts the keys from the `openTxns` map, confirming that a `TreeMap` automatically maintains ascending numeric order of the transaction IDs, consistent with unsigned 32-bit integer ordering.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

